WEBVTT

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.177
Pip, the coffee is getting cold!

00:00:02.347 --> 00:00:08.363
Our simulated campus rover has a delivery to make, but its
familiar route is blocked.

00:00:08.533 --> 00:00:14.613
Repeating yesterday's move will not solve today's problem. Pip
needs useful feedback,

00:00:14.723 --> 00:00:16.963
and a way to change its estimates.

00:00:17.133 --> 00:00:21.421
Have you ever wondered how an agent learns which action to try?

00:00:21.591 --> 00:00:25.559
In Chapter two, Behaviorism and Behaviorist AI,

00:00:25.669 --> 00:00:30.490
we will connect the reinforcement-learning loop to one numerical
update.

00:00:32.498 --> 00:00:39.751
Today you will identify an agent, an environment, a state, an
action, a reward, and a policy.

00:00:39.921 --> 00:00:47.814
You will compare exploration with exploitation, then calculate
the example in the subtopic Update a Q value.

00:00:47.984 --> 00:00:53.168
Welcome to the tutorial on basic reinforcement learning by Blue
Impulse.

00:00:53.338 --> 00:01:01.168
Have paper ready for one calculation. The tutorial PDF includes
the same worked steps and practice question.

00:01:01.338 --> 00:01:05.988
Treat our rover as an illustration. We are explaining a learning
method,

00:01:06.098 --> 00:01:09.298
not claiming every real rover learns this way.

00:01:11.628 --> 00:01:15.639
Start with the subtopic The reinforcement-learning loop. One

00:01:15.749 --> 00:01:20.101
interaction connects an observation, an action, and feedback.

00:01:20.271 --> 00:01:26.927
The agent selects an action. The environment changes and returns
the next state and a reward.

00:01:27.097 --> 00:01:34.798
The learning algorithm uses that experience to improve the
policy, which is the strategy for choosing actions.

00:01:34.968 --> 00:01:40.366
For our grid, the state could be the current square, and an
action could be moving right.

00:01:40.536 --> 00:01:47.768
The reward is a number associated with the result. It is
feedback, not a complete instruction manual.

00:01:47.938 --> 00:01:53.932
Notice the return arrow. Learning depends on connecting an
action to what happened after it.

00:01:54.102 --> 00:01:58.412
For Pip, define the state before choosing an action. A location

00:01:58.522 --> 00:02:02.447
alone may be insufficient if a barrier can change the route.

00:02:02.617 --> 00:02:09.060
The environment includes what happens after the action. The
reward is feedback about that outcome,

00:02:09.170 --> 00:02:12.050
not a replacement for observing the next state.

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:21.744
The subtopic Rewards, policies and values separates three ideas
that are easy to confuse.

00:02:21.914 --> 00:02:28.272
Think of a journey. The reward scores an outcome now, while the
policy chooses the next move.

00:02:28.442 --> 00:02:34.628
A value estimate predicts how much return a choice may lead to,
including later outcomes.

00:02:34.798 --> 00:02:41.156
A route with a small cost now can still be worthwhile if it
reaches a better destination.

00:02:41.326 --> 00:02:47.811
That is why choosing the biggest immediate reward is not always
the best long-term decision.

00:02:47.981 --> 00:02:54.146
A poorly chosen reward can encourage a shortcut that misses the
designer's real goal.

00:02:54.316 --> 00:03:01.420
Suppose the reward gives a point for moving, regardless of
direction. Pip could keep moving in circles

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:04.538
and collect points without completing a delivery.

00:03:04.708 --> 00:03:08.143
An objective and a reward design are connected,

00:03:08.253 --> 00:03:12.136
but they are not automatically identical. Test whether the

00:03:12.246 --> 00:03:16.171
rewarded behaviour is the behaviour you actually wanted.

00:03:19.454 --> 00:03:23.870
Use a restaurant to understand the subtopic Explore or exploit?

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:31.635
Ordering the dish you currently believe is best is exploitation.
Trying a different dish is exploration.

00:03:31.805 --> 00:03:37.309
Exploration gathers information because your current estimates
might be wrong.

00:03:37.479 --> 00:03:45.586
In our epsilon-greedy example, epsilon is zero point one, giving
a ten percent exploration probability.

00:03:45.756 --> 00:03:50.598
Otherwise, the agent chooses an action with the highest current
Q value.

00:03:50.768 --> 00:03:54.800
These percentages describe probabilities over choices,

00:03:54.910 --> 00:03:59.732
not a promise that exactly one out of every ten choices
explores.

00:03:59.902 --> 00:04:02.035
In a real physical system,

00:04:02.145 --> 00:04:08.886
exploration also needs safety constraints. A simulation is a
useful place to experiment.

00:04:11.628 --> 00:04:17.282
Pause and think. One route has worked twice. Another route has
never been tried.

00:04:17.452 --> 00:04:23.062
Does that prove the first route is better? Take a few seconds
before we reveal the answer.

00:04:28.232 --> 00:04:34.184
No. The evidence is incomplete, so the familiar route is not
certainly the better one.

00:04:34.354 --> 00:04:42.226
Exploration may improve the estimate, but it has a cost. The
design must balance information and performance.

00:04:42.396 --> 00:04:50.780
Write one sentence explaining why exploitation uses estimated
future return, rather than only the next reward.

00:04:53.367 --> 00:05:01.538
Now use the subtopic Update a Q value. Q associates a state and
an action with an estimated return.

00:05:01.708 --> 00:05:09.132
Q-learning is called model-free because it does not require a
supplied model of environment transitions.

00:05:09.302 --> 00:05:16.555
Model-free does not mean memory-free. The estimates still live
in a table or another representation.

00:05:16.725 --> 00:05:22.187
The update combines an old estimate with a target derived from
new experience.

00:05:22.357 --> 00:05:30.165
Alpha controls how far the estimate moves toward that target.
Gamma discounts the future-value contribution.

00:05:30.335 --> 00:05:36.009
We will keep every input visible, so you can reproduce the
arithmetic yourself.

00:05:38.759 --> 00:05:44.881
Start with an old Q value of two and a learning rate, alpha, of
zero point five.

00:05:45.051 --> 00:05:50.961
The reward is one, gamma is zero point nine, and the best next Q
value is four.

00:05:51.131 --> 00:05:58.341
First calculate the target: one plus zero point nine times four.
The target is four point six.

00:05:58.511 --> 00:06:05.445
Next subtract the old estimate. Four point six minus two gives
an error of two point six.

00:06:05.615 --> 00:06:11.140
Multiply that error by alpha. Half of two point six is one point
three.

00:06:11.310 --> 00:06:17.625
Finally add the change to the old value. Two plus one point
three gives three point three.

00:06:17.795 --> 00:06:21.485
Watch the estimate move halfway toward the target. That

00:06:21.595 --> 00:06:25.649
halfway movement is what alpha equals one half means here.

00:06:25.819 --> 00:06:33.499
The supporting Python file repeats this calculation and prints
every intermediate value for you to inspect.

00:06:36.672 --> 00:06:44.266
This experience raised the estimate from two to three point
three. Now decide what that result actually means.

00:06:44.436 --> 00:06:51.498
It did not prove that the action is always best. One observation
is not a reliable policy.

00:06:51.668 --> 00:06:58.238
Repeated experience, an appropriate learning schedule, and
enough exploration are still important.

00:06:58.408 --> 00:07:04.851
There is also a special case: when the next state is terminal,
the future-value term is zero.

00:07:05.021 --> 00:07:11.016
That prevents the calculation from inventing future reward after
the episode has ended.

00:07:11.186 --> 00:07:18.908
Compare these two ideas: updating an estimate from evidence, and
guaranteeing the outcome of the next action.

00:07:19.078 --> 00:07:23.878
They are different claims, and only the first follows from our
calculation.

00:07:24.048 --> 00:07:29.851
Imagine that the next trial gives different feedback. One sample
is noisy evidence,

00:07:29.961 --> 00:07:32.969
so another update can move the estimate again.

00:07:33.139 --> 00:07:37.363
The learning rate controls how strongly this experience changes
the

00:07:37.473 --> 00:07:42.422
estimate. It does not turn a single successful delivery into a
guarantee.

00:07:46.063 --> 00:07:51.738
Your turn. Keep the old value at two, alpha at one half, and the
reward at one.

00:07:51.908 --> 00:07:58.244
Make the next state terminal. What is the new Q value? Pause the
video if you need more time.

00:08:04.414 --> 00:08:11.944
The future contribution is zero, so the target is one. The error
is one minus two, or negative one.

00:08:12.114 --> 00:08:19.048
Half that error is negative one half. Add it to two, giving a
new value of one point five.

00:08:19.218 --> 00:08:23.463
The estimate falls because the observed target is lower. A

00:08:23.573 --> 00:08:27.541
learning update can decrease a value as well as increase it.

00:08:29.889 --> 00:08:37.142
Return to Pip's blocked route. The agent acts, receives
feedback, and learns a policy from experience.

00:08:37.312 --> 00:08:43.990
Reward scores an outcome. Value estimates return. Exploration
improves information,

00:08:44.100 --> 00:08:47.620
while exploitation uses the best current estimates.

00:08:47.790 --> 00:08:54.851
Our worked Q update produced three point three, and our
terminal-state practice produced one point five.

00:08:55.021 --> 00:08:57.922
The training-and-deployment distinction matters:

00:08:58.032 --> 00:09:03.046
running an already learned policy is not the same as training it
online.

00:09:03.216 --> 00:09:08.165
A fixed sensor-response rule can control a machine without
learning at all.

00:09:08.335 --> 00:09:16.292
Before moving on, explain the loop to someone else and run the
included calculation with one changed reward.

00:09:16.462 --> 00:09:20.430
Pip's improvement is a change in an estimate and, possibly,

00:09:20.540 --> 00:09:27.516
a later choice. It is not proof that every new street or
unexpected obstacle has been solved.

00:09:30.933 --> 00:09:34.687
Pip's small problem now has an explanation you can test.

00:09:34.857 --> 00:09:42.580
Use Chapter 2, Behaviorism & Behaviorist AI, and the named
subtopics to revisit the reasoning.

00:09:42.750 --> 00:09:46.163
The tutorial guide includes the full teaching steps

00:09:46.273 --> 00:09:52.033
and the checked practice answers. Run the supporting
demonstration and change one input.

00:09:52.203 --> 00:09:57.537
Blue Impulse. Follow the signal, predict the result, and check
your explanation.
